WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
Structures suggest the channel gains hundreds of square nanometres.

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.140
A two-state reading of the current says about five.

3
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.420
Do they describe the same change?

4
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.830
Yang and colleagues put purified mouse Piezo1 into lipid vesicles.

5
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.870
Cryo electron microscopy sorted it into two classes, from one sample.

6
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:26.093
One class was curved, the other flattened.

7
00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:31.193
Between them was about three hundred square nanometres of in-plane expansion.

8
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:36.773
Their abstract hedges whether this rearrangement is the gating transition itself.

9
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:41.783
Suppose all three hundred square nanometres were coupled to opening.

10
00:00:42.723 --> 00:00:44.283
How steep would the current curve be?

11
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:56.083
Nearly vertical. The channel would go from ten to ninety percent open over a tiny

12
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.083
range of tension.

13
00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.707
The measured curve is about sixty times shallower.

14
00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:04.207
The half-activation tensions are of the same order.

15
00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:06.127
The slopes are not.

16
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.417
Geometry makes a change that large plausible.

17
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:18.477
On paper, an idealized Piezo2 dome gains about three hundred and ten square

18
00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:19.717
nanometres when it flattens.

19
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.950
So why is the measured switch so shallow?

20
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:24.870
The question is open.

21
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.350
Here are three testable answers.

22
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:30.853
First, the dome may not be a two-state switch.

23
00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:35.773
If it flattens gradually, like a spring, one area no longer describes it.

24
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:39.503
Second, a patch curve is a population curve.

25
00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:44.063
Uneven tension across a patch could spread the half-activation tensions.

26
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:50.983
Suppose otherwise identical two-state channels sit at equilibrium and differ only by

27
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:53.463
fixed offsets in half-activation tension.

28
00:01:54.403 --> 00:01:59.083
Then averaging broadens their response, and the slope-derived area underestimates

29
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:00.563
their common opening area.

30
00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:05.023
Outside that model it is an effective area, not a guaranteed bound.

31
00:02:05.923 --> 00:02:08.423
And the published curve also pools different patches.

32
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:12.537
Third, the flattened class may not be the open channel.

33
00:02:13.337 --> 00:02:15.357
A structure is not a current recording.

34
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.827
There is one more twist.

35
00:02:18.707 --> 00:02:23.807
In an elasticity model by Haselwandter and MacKinnon, the membrane bends away around

36
00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:24.807
the dome.

37
00:02:25.217 --> 00:02:27.497
That skirt reaches well past the protein.

38
00:02:28.457 --> 00:02:31.637
So tension does work on the footprint as well as on the channel.

39
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:36.147
Their numbers are model inputs, not the structural three hundred.

40
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:38.187
Keep the two apart.

41
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:44.530
Neither five square nanometres nor hundreds has been shown to be the area gained on

42
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.730
pore opening. The structural expansion is real; which part of it couples to opening

43
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:50.570
is still open.
